---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import DocsEnd from '../../components/DocsEnd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';

const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Docs', href: '/' },
  { label: 'Core runtime', href: '/core/quorum-crate/' },
  { label: 'quorum CLI' },
];
---

<DocsLayout
  crumbs={crumbs}
  active="cli"
  toc={[
    { href: '#synopsis', label: 'Synopsis' },
    { href: '#run', label: 'Run settings' },
    { href: '#faults', label: 'Faults' },
    { href: '#limits', label: 'Limits' },
    { href: '#sensor', label: 'Radiation sensor' },
    { href: '#output', label: 'Output' },
    { href: '#resets', label: 'Resets and power cuts' },
    { href: '#opssat', label: 'On OPS-SAT' },
  ]}
>
  <article class="body">
    <Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} />
    <h1>
      The <code style="font-size: .8em; background: none; border: 0; padding: 0">quorum</code> program
    </h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Three replica processes, a voter built on the core, fault injection, and enforced resource limits. It reports what
      it delivered and what that cost.
    </p>

    <h2 id="synopsis">Synopsis</h2>
    <pre class="pre" tabindex="0">quorum [--policy shrink|tmr] [--ticks N] [--seed S]
   [--upset-rate R] [--common-mode C] [--kat-period P]
   [--fault kill|corrupt|stuck|hang|sensor:I@T | stuckbit:I@T/LOC.BIT.V] ... [--faults-file F]
   [--max-memory-mb M] [--max-cpu-seconds S] [--cpu-percent P]
   [--max-seconds S] [--reply-timeout-ms T]
   [--sensor-mb M] [--sensor-every N]
   [--log file.csv] [--progress-every N] [--summary file.txt]
   [--checkpoint file] [--checkpoint-every N]</pre>
    <p style="margin-top: 16px">An unknown option prints this list. Every run is reproducible from its seed.</p>

    <h2 id="run">Run settings</h2>
    <div class="tbl-box" tabindex="0">
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Option</th>
            <th>Default</th>
            <th>Meaning</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">--policy</td>
            <td class="m">shrink</td>
            <td>
              <code>shrink</code> or <code>tmr</code>
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">--ticks</td>
            <td class="m">10000</td>
            <td>Results to compute</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">--seed</td>
            <td class="m">1</td>
            <td>Seed for random upsets and where they land</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">--upset-rate</td>
            <td class="m">0</td>
            <td>Chance per replica per tick of a bit flip in working memory</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">--common-mode</td>
            <td class="m">0.05</td>
            <td>Share of single-replica upsets that hit both self-check runs alike</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">--kat-period</td>
            <td class="m">64</td>
            <td>Ticks between known-answer tests on the last replica</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2 id="faults">Faults</h2>
    <p>
      Schedule a fault as <code>kind:replica@tick</code>. Repeat the option, or read a schedule with{' '}
      <code>--faults-file</code>.
    </p>
    <div class="tbl-box" tabindex="0">
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Kind</th>
            <th>What happens</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">kill</td>
            <td>The replica process dies.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">hang</td>
            <td>The replica stops answering. The voter drops it after the reply timeout.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">stuck</td>
            <td>The replica returns the same wrong value from then on.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">corrupt</td>
            <td>
              One bit flips in the replica&#39;s working memory during its next computation. (In v0.2.0, a corrupt fault
              on an odd tick in self-check mode was lost. Fixed in v0.3.0.)
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">stuckbit</td>
            <td>
              <code>stuckbit:I@T/LOC.BIT.V</code>: from tick T, bit BIT of LOC is forced to V (0 or 1), in both
              self-check runs and in the known-answer test. LOC is <code>in</code> (the input, bits 0 to 63),{' '}
              <code>block</code> (the 4 KiB working block, bits 0 to 32767), <code>acc</code> (the hash accumulator, 0
              to 63) or <code>out</code> (the result, 0 to 63). Added in v0.3.0. See{' '}
              <a href="/core/bench/#kat">known-answer test coverage</a>.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">sensor</td>
            <td>
              A flip in the radiation-sensor block, to test logging and repair. Needs <code>--sensor-mb</code>. The
              replica number is ignored.
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2 id="limits">Limits</h2>
    <p>
      Safe on shared hardware. Each process enforces its own memory and CPU-time limits. The voter enforces the CPU
      share and the run time.
    </p>
    <div class="tbl-box" tabindex="0">
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Option</th>
            <th>Default</th>
            <th>Meaning</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">--max-memory-mb</td>
            <td class="m">128</td>
            <td>Memory limit per process (Linux only)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">--max-cpu-seconds</td>
            <td class="m">off</td>
            <td>CPU-time limit per process</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">--cpu-percent</td>
            <td class="m">off</td>
            <td>Cap on the voter&#39;s CPU share</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">--max-seconds</td>
            <td class="m">off</td>
            <td>Maximum run time</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">--reply-timeout-ms</td>
            <td class="m">2000</td>
            <td>Drop a replica that does not answer in time</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2 id="sensor">Radiation sensor</h2>
    <p>
      <code>--sensor-mb M</code> holds M MB of memory in a known pattern and checks it every <code>--sensor-every</code>{' '}
      ticks (default 1000). Every flipped bit is logged with its location, then repaired. On flight hardware, this
      measures the real upset rate. It must leave at least 32 MB under <code>--max-memory-mb</code>. In orbit it found
      one flip, the injected self-test, and no real upset.
    </p>

    <h2 id="output">Output</h2>
    <p>
      <code>--log file.csv</code> writes every event: stuck, hang, lost, retired, cleared, killed, detected, wrong,
      stopped, halted and sensor flips. <code>--progress-every N</code> adds running totals.{' '}
      <code>--summary file.txt</code> writes the final report for downlink.
    </p>
    <p>
      The run from the <a href="/#quickstart">Quickstart</a>,{' '}
      <code>--policy shrink --ticks 20000 --seed 1 --upset-rate 0.001 --fault kill:0@5000 --fault kill:1@10000</code>,
      prints:
    </p>
    <pre class="pre" tabindex="0">policy=shrink ticks=20000 seed=1 upset_rate=0.001
<span class="s">useful=14989</span> detected=11 wrong=0 halted_at=never alive_at_end=1 stopped=completed last_useful=20000 kat_tests=174
footprint: voter_max_rss=1936KiB replica_max_rss=1824KiB cpu=0.429s wall=0.420s
limits: memory=128MB/process cpu_time=none cpu_share=none max_run=none reply_timeout=2000ms</pre>
    <p class="tiny" style="margin-top: 10px">
      flight v0.3.1 on an Apple M4. The footprint line varies by host.
    </p>
    <p style="margin-top: 16px">
      <code>last_useful</code> is the tick of the last correct result. With a scheduled stuck, hang or corrupt fault,
      the output adds a <code>detection:</code> line (also for <code>stuckbit</code>, since v0.3.0): the faults
      injected, how many the voter caught, and the mean and longest ticks from fault to first reaction. The{' '}
      <a href="/core/bench/">bench</a> runs this program over a matrix of faults.
    </p>

    <h2 id="resets">Resets and power cuts</h2>
    <p>
      <code>--checkpoint file</code> saves the run&#39;s state every <code>--checkpoint-every</code> ticks (default
      100). The voter writes a new file, flushes it to storage and renames it over the old one, so a power cut leaves
      either the old checkpoint or the new one. It writes out and syncs the event log first.
    </p>
    <p>
      After a reset, the same command resumes from the checkpoint. Dead, stuck and hung replicas stay that way. The
      ticks after the last checkpoint run again and give the same results, so the run ends with the same counts as one
      that was never interrupted. A <code>resumed</code> line in the log marks where it picked up. A checkpoint from a
      run with other options is refused. A finished run removes its checkpoint.
    </p>
    <p>
      The scenario checks test this on every change: they kill the voter with <code>kill -9</code> in the middle of a
      run and compare the counts. <code>scripts/board.sh</code> runs the same test on a board, with reboots or power
      cuts.
    </p>

    <h2 id="opssat">On OPS-SAT</h2>
    <p>
      For experiment exp4051 on ESA&#39;s OPS-SAT PRETTY satellite, run by ESA&#39;s OPS-SAT Space Lab and TU Graz. The
      same v0.3.1 package ran in orbit on 18 October 2026 at 09:45 UTC, after ESA&#39;s software environment test (15
      October) and the TU Graz FlatSat test (16 October). Results matched the prediction written before the run: 227 s
      in total, both runs exit 0, every fault on its scheduled tick, the shrinking quorum 2,745 correct and 0 wrong over
      5,250 ticks, fixed TMR 239 correct then a detected disagreement on every tick to 4,500. Three software replicas on
      one processor in user space, scheduled faults, no real upset in the window; not a hardware-redundancy test.
    </p>
    <div class="tbl-box" tabindex="0">
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Run</th>
            <th>Host</th>
            <th>Shrinking quorum</th>
            <th>Fixed TMR</th>
            <th>Total</th>
            <th>Exit</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Software environment, 15 Oct 2026</td>
            <td>ESA Linux VM, ARMv8, two cores</td>
            <td class="m">14,886 correct, 0 wrong, 29,532 ticks</td>
            <td class="m">239 correct, detected to tick 19,250</td>
            <td class="m">222 s</td>
            <td class="m">0, 0</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>FlatSat, 16 Oct 2026</td>
            <td>SEPP, Cortex-A9, TU Graz</td>
            <td class="m">2,745 correct, 0 wrong, 5,250 ticks</td>
            <td class="m">239 correct, detected to tick 4,778</td>
            <td class="m">224 s</td>
            <td class="m">0, 0</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>In orbit, 18 Oct 2026</td>
            <td>SEPP, Cortex-A9, OPS-SAT PRETTY</td>
            <td class="m">2,745 correct, 0 wrong, 5,250 ticks</td>
            <td class="m">239 correct, detected to tick 4,500</td>
            <td class="m">227 s</td>
            <td class="m">0, 0</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="tiny">
      Counts from the summary files and totals from the start and end times in run-info.txt, in the
      <a href="https://github.com/mruspace/flight/tree/main/opssat/results">result files</a>. The same binary ran on all
      three hosts; the software environment is a Linux VM, not the flight processor. Fixed TMR delivered 0 wrong results
      on every host; its last correct result is tick 239, then a detected disagreement on each tick to the tick shown.
      Both runs overran the 110 s cap by about 3 s because the limit is checked once per tick and the last tick was a 64
      MB scan; fixed in v0.4.0, where the scan checks the limit every 1 MiB and the sleep stops at the cap.
    </p>
    <p style="margin-top: 16px">
      On the flight processor at the 5% CPU share the voter made about 45 ticks per second on the FlatSat, and in orbit
      about 46 for the shrinking quorum and 40 for fixed TMR (
      <a href="https://github.com/mruspace/flight/blob/main/docs/opssat.md">operations note</a>).{' '}
      <code>opssat/run</code> is the entry point the OPS-SAT runner executes: the shrinking quorum and fixed TMR one
      after the other, same seed and fault schedule, all output in a folder for downlink, finished inside a five-minute
      window. <code>opssat/package.sh</code> builds the package from a checksum-verified release binary.{' '}
      <code>opssat/run.sh</code> starts, checks and stops a long run on any Linux system. Tagged releases ship each
      static Linux binary (three ARM, one x86_64) with both launchers, the fault schedules and the operations note.
    </p>
    <DocsEnd />
  </article>
</DocsLayout>
